// What each country's flag means: where its colours and symbols come from, who
// designed it and when it was adopted. One short, hand-written paragraph per
// country, shown on the /flags/<code>/ pages (scripts/prerender.mjs). Meanings
// follow the official or most widely cited explanation; where a reading is only
// popular or traditional, the text says so.
//
// Keep to facts that can be checked on Wikipedia or the country's own
// government sources. Not imported by the app, so it adds nothing to the bundle.

export const FLAG_MEANINGS: Record<string, string> = {
  // ── Europe ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  AL: "The red field and black double-headed eagle come from the seal of Gjergj Kastrioti Skanderbeg, the 15th-century noble who led Albanian resistance against the Ottoman Empire. The eagle's two heads are often read as looking east and west. Albanians call themselves “sons of the eagle”, and the country's own name, Shqipëria, is popularly linked to shqiponjë, the Albanian word for eagle. The current version, without the communist star, was adopted in 1992.",
  AD: "Andorra's blue, yellow and red recall its two co-princes: the President of France and the Bishop of Urgell in Catalonia. The coat of arms combines the bishop's mitre and crozier, the red and gold stripes of the counts of Foix and of Catalonia, and the two cows of Béarn, with the motto “Virtus Unita Fortior”, strength united is stronger. The flag's centre band is slightly wider than the other two.",
  AT: "Legend says Duke Leopold V's white tunic was soaked in blood at the Siege of Acre in 1191 except under his belt, leaving a red-white-red pattern. In fact the colours are the arms of the Babenberg dukes, recorded from the 13th century, which makes this one of the oldest national flag designs still in use. The state flag adds the federal eagle holding a sickle and hammer, with the broken chains added in 1945 to mark liberation.",
  BY: "Belarus's red and green flag was adopted after a 1995 referendum called by President Alexander Lukashenko, replacing the white-red-white flag used after independence in 1991. It is based on the flag of the Byelorussian Soviet Republic without the hammer and sickle. Red is usually linked to the defeat of invaders and green to forests and fields, while the hoist pattern is a traditional rushnyk embroidery design. The white-red-white flag is still flown by the opposition.",
  BE: "Belgium's colours come from the arms of the Duchy of Brabant: a golden lion with red claws and tongue on a black field. They were used in the Brabant Revolution of 1789 and again in the 1830 revolution that won independence from the Netherlands. The vertical layout, adopted in 1831, was probably inspired by the French tricolour. The official 13:15 proportions are unusual and rarely followed; most Belgian flags are made at 2:3.",
  BA: "The flag was imposed in 1998 by the international High Representative after the country's politicians could not agree on one, and it deliberately avoids the symbols of any single ethnic group. The yellow triangle echoes the shape of the country and its three points stand for the three constituent peoples: Bosniaks, Croats and Serbs. The blue and the line of white stars, cut off at the top and bottom to suggest they continue, refer to Europe.",
  BG: "White is usually said to stand for peace, green for the land and its farming, and red for the courage of the people and the blood shed for freedom. The tricolour was first raised during the struggle against Ottoman rule and became official in the Tarnovo Constitution of 1879, after the Russo-Turkish War. Under communism the state emblem was placed on the white stripe; it was removed in 1990.",
  HR: "Red, white and blue are the Pan-Slavic colours, taken up during the Croatian national revival of the 1840s. The shield shows the red-and-white checkerboard, the šahovnica, a Croatian symbol since at least the late 15th century. Above it sits a crown of five small shields for historic regions: the oldest known Croatian arms, the Republic of Dubrovnik, Dalmatia, Istria and Slavonia. The current flag was adopted in December 1990.",
  CY: "Adopted at independence in 1960, the flag was designed to be neutral between the Greek and Turkish Cypriot communities, so it avoids the blue and white of Greece and the red of Turkey. The island's outline is coloured copper-orange, a nod to the copper Cyprus has been known for since antiquity and which may give the island its name. The two olive branches stand for peace between the communities.",
  CZ: "White and red are the old colours of the Kingdom of Bohemia. The blue wedge was added in 1920 to represent Moravia and Slovakia within Czechoslovakia, and to tell the flag apart from Poland's. When Czechoslovakia split in 1993, the Czech Republic kept the flag even though the two states had agreed that neither would use federal symbols. Slovakia adopted its own flag instead.",
  DK: "The Dannebrog is said to have fallen from the sky during the Battle of Lyndanisse in Estonia in 1219, turning the battle for the Danes. Historians date the earliest clear record of it to the 14th century, but it is still regarded as the oldest national flag in continuous use. Its off-centre cross became the model for the Nordic cross flags of Sweden, Norway, Finland, Iceland and the Faroe Islands.",
  EE: "Blue, black and white were first the colours of the Estonian Students' Society, whose flag was consecrated in Otepää in 1884. Blue is often read as the sky, loyalty and hope; black as the dark soil and the hardships of the past; and white as snowy winters and the striving for freedom. Banned under Soviet rule, the flag was raised again on Tall Hermann tower in Tallinn on 24 February 1989.",
  FI: "The blue cross on white was proposed in the 19th century, when the poet Zachris Topelius wrote of the blue of Finland's lakes and the white of its snow, and it was adopted in May 1918 after independence from Russia. The state flag adds the coat of arms, a crowned lion standing on a sabre, at the centre of the cross. Finland's flag days include Midsummer and Independence Day on 6 December.",
  FR: "The tricolour came out of the French Revolution: blue and red were the colours of Paris, and white was long associated with the monarchy. It became the national flag in 1794. The restored Bourbon kings replaced it with a white flag, but it returned for good after the July Revolution of 1830. The shade of blue has varied over the years, and the darker navy blue was brought back at the Élysée Palace in 2020.",
  DE: "Black, red and gold trace back to the uniforms of the Lützow Free Corps that fought Napoleon, and became the colours of the 1848 revolutionaries who wanted a united, democratic Germany. The Weimar Republic adopted the flag in 1919 and the Nazi regime abolished it. Both West and East Germany restored it in 1949, the East later adding its hammer-and-compass emblem, and reunified Germany has flown the plain tricolour since 1990.",
  GR: "The nine stripes are traditionally said to match the nine syllables of the independence-war motto “Eleftheria i Thanatos”, Freedom or Death. The white cross in the canton stands for Greek Orthodox Christianity, and the blue and white are popularly linked to the sea and the sky. Used at sea since 1822, the striped design became the sole national flag in 1978, replacing a plain white cross on blue that had been used on land.",
  HU: "The colours come from the historic Hungarian coat of arms, with its red and white bars and a green triple mound. They are popularly read as strength (red), faithfulness (white) and hope (green). The tricolour took its present form during the revolution of 1848. During the 1956 uprising, protesters cut the communist emblem out of the centre, and the flag with a hole became the symbol of the revolt.",
  IS: "Iceland's flag was approved by the Danish king in 1915 and became the national flag of the republic in 1944. Blue stands for the mountains and the sea, white for snow and ice, and red for the volcanic fires of the island, so the flag is often described as fire and ice. As on the other Nordic flags, the cross is set towards the hoist.",
  IE: "Green represents the Gaelic and Catholic tradition, orange the Protestant supporters of William of Orange, and the white between them a lasting peace between the two. The tricolour was presented by Thomas Francis Meagher in 1848, inspired by the flags of revolutionary France, and was widely flown after the 1916 Easter Rising. The 1937 constitution confirmed it as the national flag.",
  IT: "The Italian tricolour was first adopted by the Cispadane Republic in 1797, modelled on the French flag carried by Napoleon's armies; green is said to come from the uniforms of the Milanese civic guard. During unification and under the monarchy it carried the arms of the House of Savoy, and the plain tricolour was adopted when Italy became a republic in 1946. A popular reading links the colours to hope, faith and charity.",
  XK: "Kosovo's flag was chosen in 2008 at independence from a design competition, and it avoids the national symbols of both Albania and Serbia. It shows the country's outline in gold on blue, colours chosen to echo the flag of Europe. The six white stars stand for the main communities living in Kosovo: Albanians, Serbs, Turks, Gorani, Roma and Bosniaks.",
  LV: "The carmine-white-carmine flag is linked to a 13th-century account in the Rhymed Chronicle of Livonia, which describes a red banner with a white stripe. Legend says a wounded chieftain was wrapped in a white sheet whose edges were stained with his blood. The flag was adopted in 1922, banned under Soviet rule and restored in 1990. Its dark shade of red is often called Latvian red.",
  LI: "Blue and red were the colours of the princely household's livery. The gold crown was added in 1937 after Liechtenstein's team discovered at the 1936 Berlin Olympics that their flag was identical to Haiti's. The crown, the princely hat, stands for the unity of the people and their prince.",
  LT: "Yellow stands for the sun and prosperity, green for the forests and hope, and red for courage and the blood shed for the country. The tricolour was adopted in 1918 when Lithuania declared independence, banned during the Soviet occupation and legalised again in 1988. Lithuania also has a historical state flag showing the white knight, Vytis, on red, which flies at a few official sites.",
  LU: "Red, white and blue come from the coat of arms of the House of Luxembourg, a red lion on blue and white stripes. The flag was only formally adopted by law in 1972. It looks almost the same as the Dutch flag, but is longer and uses a lighter blue. Over the years some politicians have proposed replacing it with the red lion flag, which remains popular with Luxembourgers.",
  MT: "White and red are traditionally linked to Count Roger I of Sicily, who is said to have given the Maltese these colours in 1091, though the story is a legend. The George Cross was awarded to the island by King George VI in 1942 for the bravery of its people during the Axis siege in the Second World War. The flag was adopted at independence in 1964.",
  MD: "Moldova shares Romania's blue, yellow and red, a sign of their common history and language. The coat of arms shows an eagle holding an Orthodox cross in its beak, an olive branch and a sceptre, with a shield bearing the head of an aurochs, the ancient symbol of the Principality of Moldavia, between a rose, a crescent and a star. The flag was adopted in 1990.",
  MC: "Red and white have been the colours of the House of Grimaldi, which has ruled Monaco since the 13th century, and come from its red-and-white lozenges. The current flag was adopted in 1881. It is nearly identical to Indonesia's, which led to a disagreement when Indonesia adopted its flag in 1945; neither country changed, and the two differ only in proportions.",
  ME: "Montenegro's flag, adopted in 2004, shows the double-headed golden eagle of the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty on a red field with a golden border. The eagle holds a sceptre and an orb, and on its breast is a shield with a golden lion, an old symbol of the prince-bishops who once ruled Montenegro. The country became independent from its union with Serbia two years later, in 2006.",
  NL: "The Dutch flag descends from the orange, white and blue “Prince's flag” of William of Orange, who led the revolt against Spain in the 16th century. During the 17th century orange gave way to red, possibly because orange dye faded quickly and red was easier to see at sea. The red-white-blue was formally confirmed in 1937, and an orange pennant is still flown above it on royal birthdays.",
  MK: "The golden sun with eight broadening rays on red stands for “the new sun of liberty” sung of in the national anthem. It replaced the Vergina Sun flag in 1995, after Greece objected to that ancient Macedonian symbol and imposed a trade embargo. Under the 2018 Prespa Agreement the country changed its name to North Macedonia in 2019, but kept this flag.",
  NO: "Norway's flag was designed by Fredrik Meltzer and adopted by parliament in 1821. It places a blue cross inside the white cross of Denmark's flag, and the red, white and blue were chosen as colours of liberty, associated at the time with France, the United States and Britain. Between 1844 and 1899 it carried a union mark for the union with Sweden in its canton.",
  PL: "White and red come from the coat of arms of Poland, a white eagle on a red shield, used since the Middle Ages. The flag was adopted in 1919 after Poland regained independence, and the state flag and naval ensign add the eagle to the white stripe. Poland has celebrated a national Flag Day every 2 May since 2004.",
  PT: "Green and red were adopted after the 1910 revolution that ended the monarchy; green is linked to hope and red to the blood of those who died for the nation. The yellow armillary sphere recalls the navigators of the Age of Discovery, and the shield holds seven castles and five blue shields, symbols of the Portuguese kings since the 12th century. The flag was officially adopted in 1911.",
  RO: "Blue, yellow and red came together in the principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia and were used as a tricolour in the 1848 revolution and again from 1866. Under communism the state emblem was placed in the centre. In December 1989 revolutionaries cut the emblem out, and the plain tricolour was restored. The flag is nearly identical to Chad's, which uses a darker shade of blue.",
  RU: "White, blue and red were first used on Russian merchant ships at the end of the 17th century, in the time of Peter the Great, possibly inspired by the Dutch flag. The tricolour became the national flag of the Russian Empire, was replaced by the red Soviet flag after the revolution, and was restored in 1991. A popular reading links white to nobility, blue to faithfulness and red to courage.",
  SM: "White and blue stand for the snow on Mount Titano and the sky above it, or for peace and liberty. The coat of arms shows three towers on the three peaks of Monte Titano, topped by a crown that stands for sovereignty, with the motto “Libertas”. San Marino describes itself as the world's oldest surviving republic, traditionally founded in 301 AD.",
  RS: "Red, blue and white are the Pan-Slavic colours, in the reverse order of Russia's flag. The coat of arms shows a white double-headed eagle with a shield bearing a cross and four firesteels, which are often read as four letters for the motto “Only unity saves the Serbs”. The current state flag was adopted in 2004 and redrawn in 2010.",
  SK: "Slovakia uses the Pan-Slavic white, blue and red. The coat of arms shows a white double cross standing on three blue hills, often linked to the Tatra, Fatra and Mátra mountain ranges and to the Christian mission of Saints Cyril and Methodius. The shield was added in 1992 to tell the flag apart from Russia's, and the flag has been official since September 1992.",
  SI: "Slovenia's tricolour dates from the 1848 revolution and the colours of the historic land of Carniola. The shield added in 1991 shows Mount Triglav, the country's highest peak, in white above two wavy blue lines for the sea and rivers, with three golden six-pointed stars from the arms of the Counts of Celje. It is often confused with Slovakia's flag.",
  ES: "Red and yellow come from the arms of the Crown of Aragon and were chosen by King Charles III in 1785 for Spanish warships, so they could be told apart at sea from other Bourbon kingdoms that flew white. The coat of arms combines Castile, León, Aragon, Navarre and Granada, with the Pillars of Hercules and the motto “Plus Ultra”, further beyond. The current version dates from 1981.",
  SE: "Blue and gold come from the Swedish coat of arms, three gold crowns on blue, used since at least the 14th century. The cross design, inspired by Denmark's Dannebrog, has been used since the 16th century. Sweden's National Day and Flag Day, 6 June, marks Gustav Vasa's election as king in 1523.",
  CH: "The white cross on red comes from the banner of the canton of Schwyz and from the white cross worn by Swiss Confederate soldiers from the 14th century. It became the federal flag in 1848. It is one of only two square national flags, with the Vatican's, though a 2:3 version is used at sea. The Red Cross emblem was created by reversing its colours, to honour Switzerland.",
  UA: "Blue and yellow were the colours of the coat of arms of the medieval Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia and were used by Ukrainians during the revolutions of 1848. The flag is widely read as a blue sky above golden wheat fields. It was used by the Ukrainian People's Republic in 1918, banned in Soviet times, and officially adopted again in January 1992 after independence.",
  GB: "The Union Flag combines the red cross of St George for England, the white saltire of St Andrew for Scotland, and the red saltire of St Patrick for Ireland. The first version joined England and Scotland in 1606, and St Patrick's cross was added in 1801. Wales is not represented because it was already joined to England when the first union flag was made.",
  VA: "Yellow and white recall the gold and silver keys of Saint Peter, the “keys of the kingdom of heaven” that the Gospel says Christ gave him. The crossed keys are tied with a red cord and topped by the papal tiara. The flag was adopted in 1929, when the Lateran Treaty created Vatican City, and it is one of only two square national flags, with Switzerland's.",

  // ── Africa ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DZ: "Green is linked to Islam and the land, white to peace and purity, and the red crescent and star to Islam and the blood of those who died for independence. The flag was flown by the National Liberation Front during the war of independence against France and was officially adopted when Algeria became independent on 3 July 1962. Its design is often traced to the nationalist leader Messali Hadj's movement.",
  AO: "Red stands for the blood shed during the liberation struggle and black for Africa. The yellow emblem, half a cog wheel crossed by a machete with a star, echoes the hammer and sickle: the wheel for industrial workers, the machete for farm workers and the armed struggle, and the star for international solidarity and progress. The flag is based on that of the MPLA, which took power at independence in 1975.",
  BJ: "Green stands for hope and renewal, yellow for wealth, and red for courage. The Pan-African colours were chosen in 1959, as the country, then called Dahomey, moved towards independence from France. From 1975 to 1990 the People's Republic of Benin used a green flag with a red star; the original tricolour was restored in 1990 after the end of Marxist rule.",
  BW: "The light blue stands for water, especially rain, echoing the national motto “Pula”, which means rain in Setswana. The black and white stripes represent the harmony of people of different races, and also recall the coat of the zebra, the national animal. Adopted at independence from Britain in 1966, it is one of the few African flags that does not use the Pan-African colours.",
  BF: "Red recalls the revolution and green the country's agricultural wealth; the yellow star is the guiding light of the revolution. The flag was introduced in 1984 by Thomas Sankara when he renamed Upper Volta as Burkina Faso, “land of upright people”. It replaced the black-white-red tricolour of Upper Volta, which stood for the Black, White and Red Volta rivers.",
  BI: "The white saltire and centre stand for peace, green for hope and red for the struggle for independence. The three six-pointed stars represent the country's three main ethnic groups, the Hutu, Tutsi and Twa, and also the national motto “Unity, Work, Progress”. The flag was adopted in 1967, after the monarchy was abolished.",
  CM: "Cameroon uses the Pan-African colours: green for the forests of the south, yellow for the savannas of the north, and red for unity. The yellow “star of unity” was adopted in 1975; from 1961 two stars had stood for the two federated states of formerly French and British Cameroon. The original flag of 1957 had no stars at all.",
  CV: "Cape Verde's flag, adopted in 1992, replaced a design based on the flag of the PAIGC, the independence movement it once shared with Guinea-Bissau. Blue stands for the sea and the sky, the white and red stripes for peace and for the effort of building the nation, and the ten yellow stars for the ten main islands of the archipelago.",
  CF: "The flag was designed by Barthélemy Boganda, who hoped to unite former French colonies in a larger central African state. It combines the blue, white and red of France with the Pan-African green, yellow and red, to show friendship between Europe and Africa. The vertical red band joining them stands for the blood all people share, and the yellow star for independence. It was adopted in 1958.",
  TD: "Chad's flag combines French and Pan-African colours: blue for the sky and hope, yellow for the sun and the desert of the north, and red for progress and the sacrifices made for independence. Adopted in 1959, it is almost identical to Romania's flag. The match drew international attention in 2004, but neither country changed its flag.",
  KM: "The four stripes and four stars stand for the four main islands of the archipelago: yellow for Mohéli, white for Mayotte, red for Anjouan and blue for Grande Comore. Mayotte voted to remain French but is still claimed by the Comoros. The green triangle with its white crescent stands for Islam. The current flag was adopted with a new constitution in 2001.",
  CG: "Green stands for the forests and hope, yellow for friendship and the nobility of the people, and red for the struggle for independence. The diagonal tricolour was adopted in 1959. From 1970 to 1991 the People's Republic of the Congo used a red flag with communist symbols, and the original flag was restored in 1991.",
  CD: "Sky blue represents peace, red the blood of the country's martyrs, yellow its wealth and the star a radiant future. The current flag, adopted in 2006, is based on the one used from 1963 to 1971. The country has changed its flag several times since independence in 1960, including the green Zaire flag with a hand holding a torch, used from 1971 to 1997.",
  CI: "Orange stands for the savanna of the north, white for peace and unity, and green for the forests of the south and hope for the future. The flag was adopted in 1959, a year before independence from France, and its vertical layout follows the French tricolour. It is a mirror image of Ireland's flag, though shorter in proportion.",
  DJ: "Light blue represents the Issa Somali people as well as the sea and sky, green the Afar people and the earth, white peace, and the red star the unity of the nation. It was the flag of the independence movement and was adopted when Djibouti became independent from France in 1977.",
  EG: "Egypt's flag uses the colours of the 1952 revolution: red for the time before the revolution and the struggle against colonial rule, white for the bloodless revolution itself, and black for the end of oppression. The golden Eagle of Saladin was adopted in 1984; earlier versions used two green stars, and then the Hawk of Quraish.",
  GQ: "Green represents the natural resources and forests, white peace, red the fight for independence, and the blue triangle the sea that links the mainland with the islands. The coat of arms shows a silk-cotton tree and six stars for the mainland and the five main islands, with the motto “Unidad, Paz, Justicia”. The flag was adopted at independence from Spain in 1968.",
  ER: "Green stands for agriculture and livestock, blue for the Red Sea, and red for the blood shed in the thirty-year war of independence. The gold olive wreath and branch come from the 1952 flag of Eritrea, when it was federated with Ethiopia; the branch has thirty leaves, one for each year of the war. The current flag was adopted in 1995.",
  SZ: "Blue stands for peace, yellow for the country's resources, and crimson for past battles. The centre shows a Nguni shield of black and white ox-hide with two spears and a staff decorated with tassels of widowbird feathers, symbols of the monarchy. The flag is based on a banner given to the Swazi Pioneer Corps in 1941 and was adopted in 1968.",
  ET: "Green, yellow and red have been Ethiopia's colours since the late 19th century. Because Ethiopia resisted colonisation, they became the model for the Pan-African colours used by many countries at independence. The blue disc with a yellow star, added in 1996, represents the unity and equality of the nations and peoples of Ethiopia.",
  GA: "Green stands for the forests that cover most of Gabon, yellow for the equator, which crosses the country, and the sun, and blue for the sea along its Atlantic coast. The flag was adopted in 1960 at independence from France. An earlier version from 1959 carried a small French tricolour in its corner.",
  GM: "Red stands for the sun and the savanna, blue for the Gambia River that runs through the whole length of the country, and green for forests and farming. The thin white stripes stand for peace and unity. The flag was adopted at independence from Britain in 1965 and carries no political party symbols.",
  GH: "Red recalls the blood of those who fought for independence, gold the country's mineral wealth, and green its forests and farmland. The black star, a symbol of African freedom, was taken from Marcus Garvey's Black Star Line. Designed by Theodosia Okoh, the flag was adopted in 1957 when Ghana won independence from Britain.",
  GN: "Red represents the blood of the martyrs and labour, yellow the sun and the country's gold, and green its vegetation, reflecting the national motto “Work, Justice, Solidarity”. The flag was adopted in 1958, when Guinea became the first French colony in Africa to vote for immediate independence.",
  GW: "Guinea-Bissau's flag is based on the flag of the PAIGC, the movement that fought for independence from Portugal. Red stands for the blood of the liberation struggle, yellow for the sun and wealth, green for hope, and the black star for Africa and African unity. It was adopted in 1973, when independence was declared.",
  KE: "Black represents the people of Kenya, red the blood shed in the fight for independence, and green the land and its natural wealth; the white stripes stand for peace. The Maasai shield and two spears stand for the defence of freedom. The flag is based on that of the Kenya African National Union and was adopted at independence in 1963.",
  LS: "Blue stands for the sky and rain, white for peace and green for prosperity. The black mokorotlo, the traditional conical straw hat of the Basotho, shows that Lesotho is a nation of black Africans. This design was adopted in 2006 to replace a flag with a shield, spear and club that was seen as too militaristic.",
  LR: "Liberia's flag shows its historic ties to the United States: the country was founded by the American Colonization Society as a home for freed African Americans. The eleven stripes stand for the eleven signatories of the 1847 declaration of independence, and the single white star, the Lone Star, for Liberia as the first independent republic in Africa.",
  LY: "The flag was first adopted in 1951 for the newly independent Kingdom of Libya. Its centre band and white crescent and star come from the black banner of the Senussi order of Cyrenaica, and the three bands are often linked to the historic regions of Fezzan (red), Cyrenaica (black) and Tripolitania (green). Gaddafi's plain green flag replaced it from 1977 until it was restored in 2011.",
  MG: "White stands for purity, red for sovereignty, and green for hope. Red and white were the colours of the Merina Kingdom that ruled most of the island before French colonisation, while green represents the coastal peoples. The flag was adopted in 1958, two years before independence.",
  MW: "Black stands for the people of Africa, red for the blood of martyrs for African freedom, and green for the country's ever-green nature. The rising red sun represents the dawn of hope and freedom for the whole continent. Malawi briefly replaced it from 2010 to 2012 with a flag showing a full white sun, before the original 1964 design was restored.",
  ML: "Green stands for hope and the fertile land, yellow for purity and the country's mineral wealth, and red for the blood shed for independence. Mali's first flag carried a black kanaga, a human-like figure from Dogon art, in the centre; it was removed in 1961 after objections that Islam discourages depicting human figures.",
  MR: "The green field and the gold crescent and star are symbols of Islam, and gold also stands for the sands of the Sahara. The two red stripes were added after a 2017 referendum to honour the blood shed by those who fought for independence from France. The original green flag was adopted in 1959, the year before independence.",
  MU: "The four equal stripes, known as the Four Bands, stand for the struggle for independence (red), the Indian Ocean (blue), the light of independence (yellow) and the island's farmland and evergreen vegetation (green). The flag was adopted at independence from Britain in 1968.",
  MA: "Red has been linked to Morocco's ruling Alaouite dynasty since the 17th century. The green interlaced five-pointed star, known as the Seal of Solomon, was added in 1915 under the French protectorate, and its five points are often linked to the five pillars of Islam. Before then, Morocco's plain red flag was easily confused with other flags at sea.",
  MZ: "Mozambique's is one of the few national flags to show a modern rifle. The AK-47 stands for defence and vigilance, the hoe for agriculture and the open book for education, and the yellow star for Marxism and internationalism. Green is the land, black the African continent, yellow the mineral wealth, white peace and red the struggle for independence. It was adopted in 1983, based on the FRELIMO flag.",
  NA: "Blue represents the sky, the Atlantic Ocean and the importance of water; red the people and their heroism; green the vegetation; and white peace and unity. The golden sun with twelve triangular rays stands for life and energy. The flag was adopted at independence from South Africa in 1990, combining colours of the country's main political parties.",
  NE: "Orange stands for the Sahara desert in the north, white for purity and hope, and green for the fertile south and the Niger River. The orange circle represents the sun. The flag was adopted in 1959, a year before independence from France. It resembles India's flag, which has a blue wheel in the middle instead of an orange disc.",
  NG: "Green stands for the country's forests and natural wealth and white for peace and unity. The flag was designed by Michael Taiwo Akinkunmi, a Nigerian student in London, who won a national competition in 1958; the judges removed the red sun he had placed in the centre. It was first raised at independence on 1 October 1960.",
  RW: "Light blue stands for happiness and peace, yellow for economic development, and green for hope and prosperity. The golden sun with 24 rays stands for enlightenment. The flag was adopted in 2001 to replace the red-yellow-green tricolour with a black letter R, which had become associated with the 1994 genocide against the Tutsi.",
  ST: "Green represents the islands' lush vegetation, yellow the sun and cocoa, and red the struggle for independence. The two black stars stand for the two main islands, São Tomé and Príncipe. The flag is based on that of the independence movement MLSTP and was adopted at independence from Portugal in 1975.",
  SN: "Senegal uses the Pan-African colours: green for hope and the faith of the people, yellow for wealth and the arts, and red for life and the struggle for independence. The green star stands for unity and hope. The design comes from the flag of the short-lived Mali Federation of 1959–1960, which carried a kanaga figure where the star is now.",
  SC: "The five oblique bands fanning out from the lower hoist suggest a young country moving into the future. Blue stands for the sky and sea, yellow for the sun, red for the people and their work, white for justice and harmony, and green for the land. The flag was adopted in 1996, combining the colours of the main political parties.",
  SL: "Green stands for agriculture and the mountains, white for unity and justice, and blue for the natural harbour of Freetown and the hope of peace. The flag was adopted at independence from Britain in 1961.",
  SO: "The light blue was inspired by the flag of the United Nations, which oversaw Somalia's path to independence. The white Star of Unity has five points for the five areas where Somali people live: former Italian Somaliland, former British Somaliland, the Ogaden in Ethiopia, northeastern Kenya and Djibouti. Adopted in 1954, it became the national flag in 1960.",
  ZA: "Adopted for the first fully democratic elections in 1994, the flag was designed by State Herald Frederick Brownell in a matter of weeks. It has no official meaning, but the Y shape is widely seen as different parts of society coming together and moving forward as one. It includes red, white and blue from the Boer republics and Britain, and black, green and gold from the flag of the ANC.",
  SS: "Black represents the people of South Sudan, red the blood shed for freedom, green the land, and the white stripes peace. The blue triangle stands for the waters of the Nile, and the gold star for the unity of the states of South Sudan. Originally the flag of the Sudan People's Liberation Movement, it became the national flag at independence in 2011.",
  SD: "Red, white, black and green are the pan-Arab colours. Red recalls the struggle for independence and its martyrs, white peace and optimism, black the country's name and the flag of the Mahdist revolution of the 1880s, and green Islam and farming. The flag was adopted in 1970, replacing a blue-yellow-green tricolour used since independence in 1956.",
  TZ: "Green represents the land and its vegetation, yellow the mineral wealth, black the people, and blue the many lakes and rivers and the Indian Ocean. The diagonal design combines the flags of Tanganyika and Zanzibar, which united in 1964 to form Tanzania.",
  TG: "The five stripes stand for Togo's five regions: green for hope and agriculture, yellow for faith that work brings prosperity. The red canton recalls the blood shed for independence, and the white star stands for life and purity. The flag was designed by the artist Paul Ahyi and adopted in 1960.",
  TN: "Tunisia's flag dates from the 1830s, under the Husainid beys, and closely follows the Ottoman flag. The red crescent and star inside a white disc are symbols of Islam and of unity, and red is said to represent the blood of the martyrs. It became the flag of independent Tunisia in 1956 and its exact design was fixed by law in 1999.",
  UG: "Black stands for the people of Africa, yellow for sunshine, and red for brotherhood. The grey crowned crane, chosen for its gentle nature, had been the badge of Ugandan soldiers under British rule. The flag was adopted at independence in 1962.",
  ZM: "Green stands for the land, red for the struggle for freedom, black for the people of Zambia and orange for the country's mineral wealth, above all copper. The orange African fish eagle flying above them stands for the people's ability to rise above the nation's problems. The flag was adopted at independence in 1964.",
  ZW: "Green stands for agriculture, yellow for mineral wealth, red for the blood shed in the liberation war, and black for the majority of the people. The white triangle stands for peace, the red star for the hopes of the nation, and the Zimbabwe Bird, a soapstone carving from the ancient city of Great Zimbabwe, for the country's history. It was adopted at independence in 1980.",

  // ── Asia ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  AF: "Afghanistan changed its flag more often than any other country in the 20th century. The black, red and green tricolour of the Islamic Republic (2004–2021) stands for the troubled past, the blood of those who fought for independence, and hope; its emblem shows a mosque with a mihrab and minbar framed by wheat. Since 2021 the Taliban have flown a white flag with the black Shahada, while the tricolour is still widely used by Afghans abroad.",
  AM: "According to Armenia's constitution, red stands for the Armenian Highland and the people's struggle for survival, blue for the will to live under peaceful skies, and orange for the creative talent and hard work of the people. The tricolour was first used by the First Republic of Armenia from 1918 to 1920 and was adopted again in 1990.",
  AZ: "Blue represents Azerbaijan's Turkic heritage, red progress and modern democracy, and green Islam. The white crescent and eight-pointed star are often linked to the eight branches of the Turkic peoples. First adopted in 1918 by the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic, the flag was restored in 1991.",
  BD: "The red disc represents the sun rising over Bengal and the blood of those who died in the 1971 Liberation War, and the green the lushness of the land. The disc is set slightly towards the hoist so it looks centred when the flag flies. The first version, used during the war, had a yellow map of the country inside the disc; it was removed in 1972.",
  BT: "The yellow upper half represents the secular authority of the Druk Gyalpo, the Dragon King, and the orange lower half the Buddhist spiritual tradition. The white dragon, Druk, refers to the country's own name, Druk Yul, “Land of the Thunder Dragon”, and the jewels in its claws stand for the wealth and security of the nation. The current design dates from 1969.",
  BN: "The yellow field represents the Sultan, and the black and white diagonal stripes his chief ministers. The red crest in the centre combines a royal parasol, wings for justice, peace and prosperity, two raised hands for the government's duty to its people, and a crescent for Islam, with the national motto “Always in service with God's guidance” and the words “Brunei, Abode of Peace”.",
  KH: "Cambodia's flag shows Angkor Wat, the 12th-century temple complex, in white to stand for religion and integrity, with blue for the monarchy and red for the nation. It is one of very few national flags to show a building. First adopted in 1948, it was replaced under the Khmer Rouge and the People's Republic and restored in 1993 with the return of the monarchy.",
  CN: "Red stands for the communist revolution and yellow for the golden light of the Chinese nation. The large star represents the Communist Party, and the four smaller stars, each pointing towards it, the four social classes described by Mao Zedong: workers, peasants, the petty bourgeoisie and the national bourgeoisie. The flag was designed by Zeng Liansong and adopted in 1949.",
  GE: "The Five Cross Flag is based on a medieval Georgian banner with a large red cross of St George, Georgia's patron saint, and four smaller crosses in the corners. The design was revived by the National Movement in the 1990s and became the official flag in 2004, after the Rose Revolution, replacing the dark red flag of the 1990s.",
  IN: "Saffron stands for courage and sacrifice, white for truth and peace, and green for faith, fertility and prosperity. The navy blue Ashoka Chakra, a 24-spoke wheel taken from the Lion Capital of Ashoka at Sarnath, represents the wheel of law, dharma. The flag was adopted by the Constituent Assembly on 22 July 1947, shortly before independence, and was traditionally made from khadi, hand-spun cloth.",
  ID: "Red and white, the Sang Saka Merah-Putih, are linked to the 13th-century Majapahit Empire, whose banners used the same colours. Red stands for courage and white for purity. The flag was first officially raised at the proclamation of independence on 17 August 1945; that original flag, sewn by Fatmawati Sukarno, is kept as a national heirloom.",
  JP: "The Hinomaru, “circle of the sun”, reflects Japan's name, Nihon, often translated as “origin of the sun”. A red sun disc was used on fans and banners for centuries before it became the flag of Japanese merchant ships in 1870. It only became the legal national flag in 1999, when a law also fixed the disc at the exact centre.",
  KZ: "The sky blue stands for the Turkic peoples and the sky, and for peace and unity. The golden sun with 32 rays represents life and abundance, and the steppe eagle beneath it freedom and the state. The pattern along the hoist is a traditional Kazakh ornament known as “ram's horns”. Designed by Shaken Niyazbekov, the flag was adopted in 1992.",
  KG: "The red field is said to come from the banner of the epic hero Manas. The yellow sun has 40 rays for the 40 tribes Manas united, and at its centre is the tunduk, the wooden crown of a yurt, a symbol of home, family and the universe. The flag was adopted in 1992, and in 2023 the rays were straightened and the tunduk redrawn.",
  LA: "The red stripes stand for the blood shed in the struggle for independence, the blue for the country's wealth, and the white disc for the full moon over the Mekong River and the unity of the people. First used by the Lao Issara movement in 1945, it became the national flag in 1975 when the monarchy was abolished. It is unusual among communist states' flags in having no star.",
  MY: "The fourteen stripes, the Jalur Gemilang or “Stripes of Glory”, stand for the equal status of the thirteen states and the federal government, as do the fourteen points of the star. The crescent represents Islam, yellow the Malay rulers, and the blue canton the unity of the people. The flag first had eleven stripes in 1950; three were added in 1963 when Sabah, Sarawak and Singapore joined.",
  MV: "Red represents the courage of the nation's heroes, green peace and prosperity, and the white crescent the Islamic faith. The earliest flags of the Maldivian sultans were plain red; the green rectangle and crescent were added in the early 20th century, and the current flag dates from 1965, when the country became independent from Britain.",
  MN: "Blue stands for the “eternal blue sky” revered in Mongolian tradition, and red for progress and prosperity. The yellow Soyombo symbol combines fire, sun, moon, earth, water and the yin-yang, and stands for freedom and independence. The communist star above it was removed in 1992.",
  MM: "Yellow stands for solidarity, green for peace and the land, and red for courage and decisiveness. The large white star represents the union of the country. The flag was adopted in 2010 under a new constitution, replacing a red flag with a blue canton showing a cogwheel and ears of rice.",
  NP: "Nepal's double pennant is the only non-rectangular national flag. Its crimson is the colour of the rhododendron, the national flower, and the blue border stands for peace. The moon and the sun express the hope that the nation will last as long as they do. The 1962 constitution includes step-by-step geometric instructions for drawing it.",
  KP: "The red stripe represents the revolutionary traditions and patriotism, the blue stripes the desire for peace and unity, and the white the purity of the nation. The red star in a white disc represents socialism. The flag replaced the Taegukgi in 1948, when the Democratic People's Republic of Korea was proclaimed.",
  PK: "Dark green represents the Muslim majority and the white stripe the country's religious minorities. The crescent stands for progress and the five-pointed star for light and knowledge. Based on the flag of the All-India Muslim League, it was adopted on 11 August 1947, three days before independence.",
  PH: "Blue stands for peace, truth and justice, red for patriotism and valour, and the white triangle for equality and brotherhood. The sun's eight rays represent the first eight provinces that rose against Spain in 1896, and the three stars Luzon, the Visayas and Mindanao. In time of war the flag is flown with the red stripe on top.",
  SG: "Red stands for universal brotherhood and equality of all people, and white for purity and virtue. The crescent represents a young nation on the rise, and the five stars the ideals of democracy, peace, progress, justice and equality. The flag was adopted in 1959, when Singapore became self-governing, and kept at independence in 1965.",
  LK: "The golden lion holding a sword comes from the flag of the last Sinhalese kingdom, Kandy. The four bo leaves stand for the Buddhist virtues of loving kindness, compassion, sympathetic joy and equanimity. The green and orange stripes, added in 1951, represent the Muslim and Tamil communities, and the bo leaves were added in 1972.",
  KR: "The Taegukgi's white background stands for purity and peace, the red and blue taegeuk at the centre for the balance of opposing forces in the universe, and the four black trigrams from the I Ching for heaven, earth, fire and water. The design dates from the 1880s and was standardised by the Republic of Korea in 1949.",
  TW: "The “Blue Sky, White Sun and a Wholly Red Earth” flag was adopted by the Republic of China in 1928 and has flown over Taiwan since 1945. The blue sky and white sun were designed by Lu Haodong for the revolutionary movement in 1895; the sun's twelve rays stand for the twelve months and the twelve traditional Chinese hours. Blue, white and red are linked to Sun Yat-sen's Three Principles of the People. At the Olympics Taiwan competes as Chinese Taipei under a different flag.",
  TJ: "Red stands for the unity of the nation, white for cotton, the country's main crop, and purity, and green for its fertile valleys. The crown and seven stars refer to the name Tajik, popularly linked to taj, crown; seven is a lucky number in Tajik tradition. The flag was adopted in 1992.",
  TH: "The Trairanga, “tricolour”, was adopted in 1917 by King Vajiravudh. Red represents the nation, white religion, and blue the monarchy. Earlier, Siam's flag showed a white elephant on red; a popular story says the king saw an elephant flag hung upside down during a flood and wanted a design that could never be.",
  TL: "Yellow stands for the traces of colonialism, black for the obscurantism that must be overcome, red for the struggle for liberation, and the white star for the light that guides the way to peace. The flag was first used in 1975 when independence from Portugal was declared, and was adopted again in 2002 when Timor-Leste became independent from Indonesia.",
  TM: "The five carpet guls in the red stripe represent the five main tribes and the country's carpet-weaving tradition, and the olive branches beneath them recall Turkmenistan's neutrality, recognised by the United Nations in 1995. The crescent stands for a bright future and the five stars for the five provinces. The flag was adopted in 1992 and took its present proportions in 2001.",
  UZ: "Blue stands for the sky and water, white for peace and purity, and green for nature; the thin red lines represent the life force. The crescent stands for the newly independent republic, and the twelve stars are said to represent the months of the year. The flag was adopted in November 1991.",
  VN: "The red field represents the revolution and the blood shed for it, and the yellow star the unity of workers, peasants, intellectuals, youth and soldiers. First used by the Viet Minh, it became the flag of North Vietnam in 1945 and of the reunified country in 1976.",

  // ── Middle East ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  BH: "Red is the traditional colour of the Gulf states. The white band was added in 1820, when Bahrain signed a treaty with Britain, to show it was at peace. The five white points of the serrated edge, fixed in 2002, stand for the Five Pillars of Islam.",
  IR: "Green represents growth and Islam, white freedom and peace, and red martyrdom and courage. The red emblem, adopted after the 1979 revolution, is a stylised form of the word Allah that also looks like a tulip, a traditional symbol of martyrs. “Allahu Akbar” is written 22 times along the edges of the stripes, for the date of the revolution's victory, 22 Bahman in the Iranian calendar.",
  IQ: "Red, white and black are pan-Arab colours, and the green writing is the Takbir, “Allahu Akbar”. The words were first added by Saddam Hussein in 1991, reportedly in his own handwriting. After his fall the script was changed to Kufic lettering, and in 2008 the three green stars of the Ba'athist design were removed.",
  IL: "The flag is based on the tallit, the Jewish prayer shawl, which is white with blue stripes. The blue Star of David, the Magen David, has been a Jewish symbol for centuries. The design was used by the Zionist movement from the late 19th century and was adopted as the flag of Israel in October 1948.",
  JO: "Based on the flag of the Arab Revolt of 1916 against Ottoman rule, its three stripes recall the Abbasid (black), Umayyad (white) and Fatimid (green) caliphates, and the red triangle the Hashemite dynasty. The seven-pointed star stands for the seven verses of the Al-Fatiha, the opening chapter of the Quran, and for the unity of the Arab peoples.",
  KW: "Green stands for the fertile land, white for the deeds of the people, red for the blood on Kuwaiti swords, and black for the battlefield. The colours come from a poem by Safi al-Din al-Hilli. The flag was adopted in 1961, replacing a red flag with the word Kuwait written on it.",
  LB: "The green cedar, the Cedar of Lebanon, stands for holiness, eternity and peace, and for the country's ancient forests, which are mentioned in the Bible. The red stripes stand for the blood shed to free the country, and the white for peace and the snow of its mountains. The flag was adopted in 1943, when Lebanon became independent from France.",
  OM: "White stands for peace and prosperity, red for battles against invaders, and green for fertility and the Green Mountain, Jebel Akhdar. The national emblem in the upper hoist shows a khanjar dagger in its sheath over two crossed swords. The flag was adopted in 1970 by Sultan Qaboos, and its proportions were changed in 1995.",
  PS: "The Palestinian flag is based on the flag of the Arab Revolt and uses the pan-Arab colours. The black, white and green stripes are linked to the Abbasid, Umayyad and Fatimid caliphates, and the red triangle to the Hashemite dynasty and the blood of martyrs. It was adopted by the Palestine Liberation Organization in 1964 and declared the flag of the State of Palestine in 1988.",
  QA: "Qatar's maroon is said to have come from natural dyes darkening in the sun, and it was officially adopted in place of red. The nine points of the serrated edge mark Qatar as the ninth member of the reconciled emirates of the Gulf after its 1916 treaty with Britain, and white stands for peace. At 11:28 it is the widest national flag.",
  SA: "The Shahada, the Islamic declaration of faith, is written in white Thuluth script: “There is no god but God; Muhammad is the Messenger of God”. The sword stands for justice and for the House of Saud. Because the text is sacred, the flag is never flown at half-mast, and it is made double-sided so that the words read correctly from both sides.",
  SY: "Syria's current flag is the independence flag, first used in 1932 under the French mandate and again from 1961 to 1963. Its green, white and black are linked to the Fatimid, Umayyad and Abbasid caliphates, and the three red stars originally stood for three regions of the country. Opposition forces adopted it during the civil war from 2011, and after the fall of Bashar al-Assad in December 2024 it replaced the red-white-black flag with two green stars used since 1980.",
  TR: "The red flag with a white crescent and star, the Ay Yıldız, comes from the late Ottoman Empire, where it was standardised in 1844. A legend says it reflects the moon and a star seen in pools of blood after a battle. The Turkish Republic set its exact proportions by law in 1936.",
  AE: "The flag uses the pan-Arab colours: green for fertility, white for neutrality, black for strength, and red for the unity of the emirates. It was designed by Abdullah Mohammed Al Maainah, then 19 years old, and first raised on 2 December 1971, when the federation was formed.",
  YE: "Red stands for the blood of martyrs and unity, white for a bright future, and black for the dark past. The flag was adopted on 22 May 1990, when North Yemen and South Yemen united, using the colours both had shared without the star and triangle that set their flags apart.",

  // ── Americas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  AG: "The rising sun stands for the dawn of a new era, black for the African heritage of most of the people, blue for hope, and red for the energy of the people. The V shape stands for victory. The flag was designed by Reginald Samuel, an art teacher, and adopted in 1967.",
  AR: "Light blue and white were worn by the patriots of the May Revolution of 1810 and turned into a flag by Manuel Belgrano in 1812. The Sun of May, a golden face with 32 rays, represents Inti, the Inca sun god, and the sun said to have broken through the clouds on 25 May 1810. Argentina's Flag Day, 20 June, marks Belgrano's death.",
  BS: "The black triangle represents the unity and determination of the Bahamian people, the aquamarine bands the clear waters around the islands, and the gold band the sun and the sandy beaches. The flag was adopted at independence from Britain in 1973.",
  BB: "The blue bands stand for the sea and sky and the gold for the sand of the beaches. The trident of Neptune is broken from its shaft to show the break from colonial rule, since the colonial badge had shown a whole trident. The flag was designed by Grantley Prescod and adopted at independence in 1966.",
  BZ: "The coat of arms shows two woodcutters with tools of the mahogany trade, a mahogany tree and the motto “Sub Umbra Floreo”, under the shade I flourish. The circle of fifty leaves recalls 1950, when the independence movement began. The red stripes were added at independence in 1981. It is one of the few national flags that shows people.",
  BO: "Red represents the blood shed by the nation's heroes, yellow its mineral wealth, and green the fertility of the land. The state flag adds the coat of arms, with the silver mountain of Potosí, an alpaca, a breadfruit tree and a sheaf of wheat. Since 2009 the Wiphala, the square chequered flag of the Andean peoples, has also been an official national symbol.",
  BR: "Green and yellow come from the royal houses of Emperor Pedro I and Empress Maria Leopoldina, though they are popularly read as forests and gold. The blue globe shows the night sky over Rio de Janeiro on 15 November 1889, when the republic was proclaimed, with 27 stars for the states and the Federal District, and the positivist motto “Ordem e Progresso”, order and progress.",
  CA: "Adopted in 1965 after a long national debate, the Maple Leaf flag replaced the Canadian Red Ensign. Red and white were proclaimed Canada's official colours by King George V in 1921, and the maple leaf had been a Canadian emblem since the 19th century. The stylised leaf has eleven points. National Flag of Canada Day is 15 February.",
  CL: "Chile's flag, La Estrella Solitaria, was adopted in 1817 during the war of independence. White stands for the snow of the Andes, blue for the sky, and red for the blood shed for independence. The star is a guide to progress and honour.",
  CO: "Yellow stands for the gold and wealth of the land, blue for the two oceans that border Colombia, and red for the blood shed for independence. The design comes from the flag of Gran Colombia created by Francisco de Miranda, which also gave rise to the flags of Ecuador and Venezuela. The current form was adopted in 1861.",
  CR: "Blue stands for the sky, opportunity and perseverance, white for peace and wisdom, and red for the blood shed for freedom and the warmth of the people. The red stripe was added in 1848, inspired by that year's revolution in France, by Pacífica Fernández, wife of President José María Castro Madriz. The civil flag has no coat of arms.",
  CU: "The three blue stripes stand for the three departments into which Cuba was divided at the time, and the two white stripes for the purity of the independence cause. The red triangle represents the blood shed for freedom and the equality of all, and the white star, La Estrella Solitaria, independence. Designed in 1849 by Narciso López and Miguel Teurbe Tolón, it was adopted in 1902.",
  DM: "The Sisserou parrot, found only on Dominica, stands for flight towards higher goals, and the ten green stars around it for the island's ten parishes. The cross of yellow, black and white represents Christianity: yellow for the soil and the island's first peoples, black for the African heritage of most Dominicans, and white for its clear rivers and waterfalls. Green stands for the forests. Adopted in 1978, it is one of only two national flags that use purple.",
  DO: "The white cross stands for the people's struggle to keep their nation and faith, blue for liberty and red for the blood of the independence war. The coat of arms shows an open Bible, making it the only national flag with a Bible, and the motto “Dios, Patria, Libertad”. The flag was designed by Juan Pablo Duarte and adopted in 1844 after independence from Haiti.",
  EC: "Ecuador shares the yellow, blue and red of Gran Colombia. Its coat of arms shows Mount Chimborazo, the Guayas River with a steamship, a sun surrounded by zodiac signs for the months of the 1845 revolution, and an Andean condor with its wings spread. The current flag was adopted in 1860.",
  SV: "The blue and white stripes come from the flag of the Federal Republic of Central America (1823–1841); the blue stands for the oceans that surrounded the federation. The coat of arms shows a triangle with five volcanoes for the former member states, a red cap of liberty and a rainbow, with the motto “Dios, Unión, Libertad”. The flag was adopted in 1912.",
  GD: "The seven stars stand for the island's seven parishes, the central one for St George's, the capital. The nutmeg recalls Grenada's nickname, the Spice Isle, and its most famous export. Red stands for courage, yellow for the sun and the warmth of the people, and green for the land. The flag was adopted at independence in 1974.",
  GT: "Sky blue stands for the two oceans and the sky, white for peace and purity. The coat of arms shows the resplendent quetzal, the national bird and a symbol of liberty, perched on a scroll with the date of independence, 15 September 1821, framed by crossed rifles, swords and laurel branches. The flag was adopted in 1871.",
  HT: "Haiti's blue and red date from the 1803 revolution, when Jean-Jacques Dessalines tore the white out of a French tricolour to show the break with France. The coat of arms shows a royal palm topped by a cap of liberty, with cannons and the motto “L'Union fait la force”, unity is strength. Haiti celebrates Flag Day on 18 May.",
  HN: "The blue stripes represent the Pacific Ocean and the Caribbean Sea, and the white the land and peace between them. The five stars stand for the five members of the former Federal Republic of Central America and the hope that they will unite again. The flag was adopted in 1866, and in 2022 Honduras changed the blue to turquoise.",
  JM: "Gold represents sunlight and natural wealth, green the land and hope, and black the strength and creativity of the people. The meaning is often summed up as “hardships there are, but the land is green and the sun shineth”. The flag was adopted at independence in 1962 and is the only national flag with none of red, white or blue.",
  MX: "The coat of arms tells the Aztec legend of the founding of Tenochtitlan: the god Huitzilopochtli told the Mexica to build their city where they saw an eagle on a cactus, eating a snake. Green, white and red date from the Army of the Three Guarantees of 1821, when they stood for independence, religion and union. The current version was adopted in 1968.",
  NI: "The blue stripes stand for the two oceans and the white for the land and peace. The coat of arms shows a triangle for equality, five volcanoes for the five members of the old Central American federation, a rising sun, a cap of liberty and a rainbow for peace. Because of the rainbow, it is one of only two national flags that use purple.",
  PA: "The quarters stand for the two political parties of the time: blue for the Conservatives and red for the Liberals, with white for peace between them. The blue star represents honesty and purity, the red star authority and law. The flag was designed by the family of Manuel Amador Guerrero, the first president, and first raised in 1903.",
  PY: "Red, white and blue were inspired by the French tricolour: red for patriotism, white for peace and blue for liberty. The front shows the national arms, a yellow star in a wreath, while the back shows the Treasury seal, a lion and a cap of liberty with the words “Paz y Justicia”. The two-sided flag dates from 1842.",
  PE: "According to legend, General José de San Martín saw a flock of flamingos with red wings and white breasts when he landed in Peru in 1820, and took their colours. Red represents the blood of the independence heroes and white peace. The vertical design was fixed in 1825.",
  KN: "Green stands for the fertile land, yellow for sunshine, black for the African heritage of the people, and red for the struggle from slavery to independence. The two white stars stand for hope and liberty. The flag was chosen in a national competition and adopted at independence in 1983.",
  LC: "Blue represents the sky and sea, gold the sunshine, and the black and white the African and European cultures living side by side. The triangles evoke the Pitons, the twin volcanic peaks that are the island's best-known landmark. The flag was designed by the artist Dunstan St Omer and adopted in 1967.",
  VC: "The three green diamonds form a V for Saint Vincent and are known as the Gems of the Antilles. Blue stands for the sky and sea, gold for sunshine and the spirit of the people, and green for the island's vegetation. The current design was adopted in 1985.",
  SR: "Green represents the fertile land, white justice and freedom, and red progress and love. The yellow star stands for unity and a golden future. The flag was adopted at independence from the Netherlands in 1975, replacing a white flag with five coloured stars for the country's main ethnic groups.",
  TT: "Red represents the vitality of the land and its people and the warmth of the sun, white the sea and the purity of the people's hopes, and black their dedication and strength. The flag was adopted at independence from Britain in 1962.",
  US: "The thirteen stripes represent the original thirteen colonies, and the fifty stars the fifty states. Red is commonly linked to valour, white to purity, and blue to vigilance and justice, a reading taken from the Great Seal. The flag has changed 27 times; the current 50-star version has flown since 4 July 1960, longer than any earlier design.",
  UY: "The nine stripes represent the nine original departments of Uruguay. The Sun of May in the canton, shared with Argentina, stands for independence. The flag was adopted in 1828 and took its present form in 1830.",
  VE: "Yellow stands for the wealth of the land, blue for the sea separating Venezuela from Spain, and red for the blood shed for independence. The stars represent the seven provinces that signed the 1811 declaration of independence; an eighth was added in 2006 following a decree by Simón Bolívar for the province of Guayana.",
  GY: "Known as the Golden Arrowhead, the flag uses green for farming and forests, white for rivers and water, gold for mineral wealth and the nation's future, black for endurance, and red for the zeal of nation-building. It was designed by the American vexillologist Whitney Smith and adopted at independence in 1966.",

  // ── Oceania ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  AU: "The Union Jack recalls the history of British settlement. The seven-pointed Commonwealth Star stands for the six federating states and the territories, and the Southern Cross is a constellation seen from every part of the country. The design won a 1901 competition with more than 32,000 entries. The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander flags have also been official flags since 1995.",
  FJ: "Fiji's light blue stands for the Pacific Ocean. The shield shows a lion holding a cocoa pod, with sugar cane, a coconut palm, bananas and a dove of peace below. The flag was adopted at independence in 1970; plans announced in 2015 to replace it and remove the Union Jack were dropped in 2016.",
  KI: "The frigate bird stands for authority, freedom and Kiribati's culture, the sun for the islands' place on the equator, and the blue and white waves for the Pacific Ocean. The three white bands are often linked to the three island groups: the Gilbert, Phoenix and Line Islands. The flag was adopted at independence in 1979.",
  MH: "The two diagonal stripes, orange for courage and white for peace, widen as they rise to show growth, and stand for the two island chains, Ratak (sunrise) and Ralik (sunset). The 24-pointed star stands for the 24 municipalities, its four longer points for the Christian cross. The flag was adopted in 1979.",
  FM: "Light blue stands for the Pacific Ocean, and the four white stars for the four states of the federation: Chuuk, Kosrae, Pohnpei and Yap. The design is based on the flag of the UN Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, which had six stars. It was adopted in 1978.",
  NR: "The yellow line represents the equator, and the white star Nauru's position just south of it. The twelve points of the star stand for the island's twelve original tribes. The flag was adopted at independence in 1968.",
  NZ: "The four red stars with white borders represent the Southern Cross, and the Union Jack the country's history as a British colony. The flag was adopted in 1902. In two referendums held in 2015 and 2016, New Zealanders voted to keep it rather than switch to a silver fern design.",
  PW: "The yellow disc represents the full moon, which Palauans regard as the best time for fishing, planting, harvesting and celebrations. The light blue stands for the Pacific Ocean and the end of foreign rule. The flag was adopted in 1981.",
  PG: "Red and black are traditional colours in the art of many Papua New Guinean peoples. The bird of paradise represents the country's emergence into nationhood, and the Southern Cross its place in the South Pacific. The flag was designed by Susan Karike, a 15-year-old schoolgirl, in 1971.",
  WS: "Red stands for courage, blue for freedom and white for purity. The Southern Cross shows Samoa's place in the South Pacific. The flag was adopted in 1949, when the fifth star was added, and kept at independence in 1962, when Samoa became the first Pacific island nation to gain independence.",
  SB: "Blue stands for the water and green for the land, and the thin yellow diagonal for sunshine. The five white stars originally stood for the five districts of the islands when the flag was adopted in 1977, the year before independence from Britain.",
  TO: "The red cross recalls Christianity and the blood of Christ. The flag was designed under King George Tupou I and adopted in 1875, and Tonga's constitution states that it may never be changed.",
  TV: "Light blue stands for the Pacific Ocean, and the nine yellow stars for the islands of Tuvalu, set out as they appear on a map. The Union Jack shows the country's links with Britain. In 1995 a new flag without the Union Jack was adopted, but it was unpopular and the original was restored in 1997.",
  VU: "Red stands for blood and the wild boar, green for the richness of the islands, and black for the Melanesian people. The yellow Y shape represents the light of the gospel spreading through the archipelago. The emblem is a boar's tusk, a symbol of prosperity, around two crossed namele leaves, a symbol of peace. The flag was adopted at independence in 1980.",
}
